sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/darwin1859-ch03-12> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Because species of the same genus are usually similar in habits and constitution and always in structure, competition between them is generally more severe than between species of different genera.";
    dc:type "empirical-generalization";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Congeners compete more severely than species of different genera";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Charles Darwin, On the Origin of Species (1st ed., London: John Murray, 1859), Chapter III, “Struggle for Existence”; section “Struggle for life most severe between individuals and varieties of the same species; often severe between species of the same genus”. Scope: co-occurring species of plants and animals"
}
